  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 302 PM EDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nFriday - Saturday...\n\nAs of Friday morning, the surface cold front will have pushed \nthrough most of the CWA and lay along portions of the KY/TN border. \nA near-stationary shortwave trough lies overhead throughout the \nweekend, leading to widespread weak showers and pulse thunderstorms \nin the overnight hours heading into Saturday. Greatest chances lie \nin the southern and eastern portions of the area. Strong storms are \nnot expected due to weak flow aloft; storms will likely struggle to \nmaintain, with the main threats being pulse-type winds. Highs will \nbe in the low to mid-80s on Friday.\n\nSaturday leads to another round of widespread showers in the morning \nbefore surface heating gets going enough to give us a chance for a \nfew isolated storms in the evening. Temps turn toward the 85-90 \ndegree mark on Saturday, with dewpoints around the 75 degree mark, \nmaking Saturday hot and muggy.\n\nSunday - Tuesday...\n\nThe upper ridge maintains as we head into early next week, leading \nto weaker surface winds and encroaching heat in the upper-80s and \nlow-90s with dewpoints once again in the upper 70s.  Realistically, \nonly a surface-level cap exists, so any cu formation has uninhibited \nvertical development opportunities. However, there also isn't a \nwhole lot of shear to work with to maintain the updraft and very \nlittle forcing exists, so only pulse-type storms should be expected. \nIsolated evening showers stay in the forecast for Sunday and Monday, \nwith better chances creeping in Tuesday night.",
